      <h2>Experience</h2>
      <p><strong>Meta</strong> | Nov 2025 to Present<br/>
      <em>Security Engineer, Agent Data Optimization (ADO), Jun 2026 to Present; Red Team X, Nov 2025 to Jun 2026</em><br/>
      Security Engineer in Meta's Applied AI (AAI) / Agent Data Optimization (ADO) organization, focused on generating high-quality cybersecurity training data for LLMs. Previously on Red Team X, Meta's former offensive security research team.</p>

      <p><strong>Stratum Security</strong> | Aug 2023 to Oct 2025<br/>
      <em>Senior Security Consultant</em><br/>
      At Stratum Security, I provided staff augmentation for a major company, conducting security reviews of high-traffic, externally facing applications. Through manual code reviews, dynamic testing, and threat modeling, my work played a critical role in safeguarding the privacy and security of millions of users worldwide.</p>

      <p><strong>Bishop Fox</strong> | Jan 2022 to Aug 2023<br/>
      <em>Security Consultant III</em><br/>
      I helped Fortune 500 companies secure their networks by performing penetration tests and security reviews of internal and external applications.</p>

      <p><strong>Independent Security Work</strong> | Jan 2019 to Present<br/>
      <em>Security Researcher</em><br/>
      I independently identified and gained recognition for finding vulnerabilities in web applications and embedded devices. I wrote comprehensive bug reports and notified vendors under a 120-day disclosure policy. I am credited with finding CVE-2020-8797, CVE-2020-8798, CVE-2020-12123, CVE-2020-12124, CVE-2020-12125, CVE-2020-12126, and CVE-2020-12127. I engaged as a member of the prestigious Synack Red Team (SRT) and contributed to the Google Vulnerability Reward Program (VRP), successfully identifying and responsibly disclosing high-risk vulnerabilities.</p>

      <p><strong>RedLattice</strong> | May 2019 to Aug 2020<br/>
      <em>Vulnerability Research Intern</em><br/>
      In the first summer, I researched and found security vulnerabilities in various commonplace embedded devices and developed tooling to exploit those vulnerabilities. In the second summer, I gained experience with JavaScriptCore and V8 vulnerability research and worked with a team to find high-impact bugs.</p>

      <p><strong>Virginia Tech IT Security Office</strong> | Sep 2019 to Dec 2020<br/>
      <em>Red Team Researcher</em><br/>
      I evaluated the security of devices on the Virginia Tech network. I developed tooling to facilitate embedded device research. I found numerous high-impact vulnerabilities, notified impacted departments, and worked with them to mitigate the issue.</p>

      <p><strong>CS @ Virginia Tech</strong> | Aug 2020 to May 2021<br/>
      <em>Undergraduate Teaching Assistant, CS 3214 (Computer Systems)</em><br/>
      I worked under the Virginia Tech Computer Science Department, fulfilling 10 hours a week as an undergraduate TA, and helped students in one of the most challenging courses in the CS curriculum, Computer Systems. I learned how to isolate bugs in student code and effectively teach systems-level concepts. I became an expert in debugging in GDB and programming in low-level C.</p>

      <p><strong>Focal Point Technologies</strong> | Jun 2017 to Aug 2018<br/>
      <em>IT Intern</em><br/>
      In the first summer, I worked on a team to develop a mobile application from scratch. In the second summer, I led a team creating embedded systems to collect data.</p>

